WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Linkage between Defect Opportunities and Probability

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.366
Welcome to this session on defect opportunities.

3
00:00:08.366 --> 00:00:11.832
Recall that we had indicated there were three fundamental rules.

4
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:18.433
First, we have to have a characteristic. In this case, what we’re going to say,

5
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:24.433
is that we have a CTQ, a critical to quality characteristic.

6
00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:30.733
Where that's an outcome. Y is a function of X. So, a CTQ is a variable.

7
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:36.133
It is a characteristic. You have to have that, to have a defect opportunity.

8
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:39.566
If you don't have a characteristic, you can't have a defect.

9
00:00:39.566 --> 00:00:42.999
Second, you gotta have a scale of measure.

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.066
If you can't quantify the CTQ, how can you have a defect?

11
00:00:48.066 --> 00:00:54.132
So, you gotta have a characteristic and you have to have a scale of measure.

12
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:57.933
Third, you have to have a standard,

13
00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:01.933
a specification limit, a performance expectation.

14
00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:06.433
In this case, we’re showing an upper spec. So, we have a characteristic,

15
00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:11.866
a performance scale of measure, and a performance expectation or standard.

16
00:01:11.866 --> 00:01:17.066
At this point we have a defect opportunity.

17
00:01:17.066 --> 00:01:22.432
But right now, it's only passive, it’s potential.

18
00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:27.466
It's not realizable yet, because there is no density.

19
00:01:27.466 --> 00:01:30.466
There's no history. There's no performance.

20
00:01:30.466 --> 00:01:36.899
It's just a scale of measure and a standard, an expectation attached.

21
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:43.066
Now, once we go out and run some parts, and operate the process,

22
00:01:43.066 --> 00:01:46.232
and create some observations.

23
00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:50.999
Now, we have opportunity, as we're producing parts.

24
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.733
Because any given part, can be above the upper spec or below the lower spec.

25
00:01:55.733 --> 00:02:01.333
Remember, a defect opportunity is a set of circumstances favorable to an end.

26
00:02:01.333 --> 00:02:04.799
Where the end is a defect opportunity. So, we have to have

27
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:11.066
a critical to quality characteristic, CTQ, a scale, and a standard.

28
00:02:11.066 --> 00:02:16.899
Now, we can in fact have an active opportunity.

29
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:21.333
Once we start measuring the parts produced for that characteristic,

30
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:24.333
in accordance to that standard.

31
00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:28.666
Those in the green is yield. Those in the red are defects.

32
00:02:28.666 --> 00:02:35.799
Thus, when I know the distribution, in relationship to the standard,

33
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:41.500
I can now see the probability of a defect.

34
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:48.466
Once I have probability, I now have a true defect opportunity.

35
00:02:48.466 --> 00:02:52.132
If the probability of a defect is zero,

36
00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:56.399
then there is no opportunity for a defect.

37
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.100
Only if that probability is greater than zero, is there an opportunity.

38
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:07.300
Now, once we start making those parts, that CTQ, then we now have a probability.

39
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:13.900
So those are the things that's required to form an active defect opportunity.

40
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:19.933
A critical to quality characteristic. A scale of measure.

41
00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:23.566
A performance expectation.

42
00:03:23.566 --> 00:03:28.899
A process distribution, density as we call it.

43
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:35.933
Those marry together to form a defect probability.

44
00:03:35.933 --> 00:03:43.066
Once I have probability, I now have the opportunity for a defect.

45
00:03:43.066 --> 00:03:49.199
Now, interestingly enough, many have a hard time understanding this concept.

46
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.666
It's easy to try to make a simple thing complex.

47
00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:59.432
So, when we talk about defect opportunities, in essence, what we’re saying is this,

48
00:03:59.433 --> 00:04:06.099
listen carefully. A defect opportunity is anything that's important to us,

49
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:12.666
that we measure. So, a CTQ is critical, it’s a characteristic, it’s a quality characteristic.

50
00:04:12.666 --> 00:04:18.366
It’s critical, therefore it's important we measure it and we report it.

51
00:04:18.366 --> 00:04:24.066
Therefore, it's a accountable defect opportunity.

52
00:04:24.066 --> 00:04:30.566
We need to account for it, as we operate our process, and accounting means reporting.

53
00:04:30.566 --> 00:04:35.232
Thus, reportable defect opportunities

54
00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:41.299
are those features, product, service, features,

55
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.833
that we feel are very important  to either us or the customer.

56
00:04:44.833 --> 00:04:48.299
For any number of reasons, that we need to measure them

57
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.300
in relationship to the standards, to report their relative quality.

58
00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:55.533
That's a defect opportunity.

59
00:04:55.533 --> 00:05:00.533
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
